        a: `The first two hours are about containment, not diagnosis.\n\nThe moment I confirm this is happening I do three things simultaneously. I call the engineering lead and ask for a feature flag or kill switch that stops new approvals for that specific segment without taking the entire product offline. I call the risk team lead and ask them to pull every approval from the last 48 hours for that segment so we understand the exposure. And I send a message to the CEO and Head of Risk saying there is an active issue, I am on it, and I will have a full picture in 90 minutes.\n\nI do not wait to fully understand the problem before I stop the bleeding. In lending, every approval that goes out in the next 30 minutes is a loan on the books that we may not be able to recover.\n\nWhile engineering is working on the kill switch I am trying to answer two questions in parallel. Did the model actually fail, meaning it encountered data it was not trained on, or did the risk team flag change after sign off and the model was never updated? And what is the regulatory exposure here? In fintech lending, approving a high risk segment incorrectly is not just a credit loss problem. Depending on the jurisdiction it can be a responsible lending compliance issue.\n\nOnce approvals are stopped, which should happen within 30 to 45 minutes if we have proper feature flags, I bring the full team together. Engineering, risk, compliance, and the data science team who built the model. The agenda is simple. What happened, what is the exposure, and what do we need to do before we turn approvals back on.\n\nThe thing I have learned in fintech is that the instinct to diagnose before acting is dangerous in a live incident. You contain first, understand second, communicate third. And you never let two hours pass without telling leadership what is happening, even if you do not have the full picture yet.`,
