        q: "Your fintech is building an account aggregator integration under the RBI AA framework. The engineering team is asking you to define the consent artifact requirements. Walk me through what you specify and why.",
        a: `The consent artifact in the AA framework is not just a technical specification. It is the legal instrument that governs every data fetch in the system, so I treat it with the same rigor I would treat a product contract.\n\nI specify seven attributes in the consent artifact and I have a reason for each one.\n\nPurpose is the first attribute. The AA framework requires explicit purpose declaration and I specify this at the most granular level the use case allows. Not just credit assessment but specifically income verification for personal loan underwriting. The narrower the purpose the lower the regulatory risk if the consent is ever challenged.\n\nFIP list is the second attribute. I define exactly which Financial Information Providers the consent covers. If we only need salary account data from a specific bank for a specific use case, I do not request consent for all linked accounts. Over requesting consent is a red flag for FIU audits and erodes user trust.\n\nData range is the third attribute. I specify the historical lookback period with precision. For a lending use case, 12 months of transaction history is typically sufficient for income verification. Requesting 36 months when 12 months serves the purpose is consent overreach.\n\nFrequency and fetch type come next. I distinguish between one time fetch for a single underwriting decision and recurring fetch for a credit monitoring product. The consent artifact must match the actual fetch pattern because every fetch is logged and auditable.\n\nExpiry is the fifth attribute. I set consent expiry to match the product lifecycle, not the maximum permitted duration. If the loan tenure is 12 months, the consent expiry is 13 months. Not five years because the framework allows it.\n\nData life and deletion policy round out the specification. I define how long we retain the fetched data and when we delete it, and I make sure this is reflected in both the consent artifact and our internal data retention policies so they do not contradict each other.\n\nThe thing I push back on engineering about most often in AA integrations is the instinct to request the maximum permitted scope at every attribute level. It feels safer from a re consent perspective but it actually increases regulatory exposure and reduces user consent acceptance rates. The consent artifact that performs best is the one that asks for exactly what you need and nothing more.`,
