        q: "You are the PM for a digital wallet product at a fintech. Monthly active users have been flat for four months despite a 30% increase in marketing spend. Your VP asks you to diagnose the problem and present a plan. What do you do?",
        a: `The first thing I tell my VP is that flat MAUs despite increased marketing spend is almost never a marketing problem. Marketing brought people in. Something in the product or the value proposition kept them from staying or coming back. I want to be clear about that framing before we go into diagnostics because if leadership thinks this is a marketing efficiency problem they will keep increasing spend and the metric will stay flat.\n\nThe diagnostic I run covers four areas.\n\nFirst I look at the full funnel from install to active. Where is the drop off sharpest? If the gap is between new user registration and first transaction, the onboarding is broken or the activation moment is not landing. If the gap is between first transaction and second transaction, the product is not delivering on whatever promise brought the user in. If the gap is between second and third transaction, the retention hooks are not working. The location of the drop off tells me where to focus.\n\nSecond I look at the definition of active we are using. MAU based on any app open is a very different number from MAU based on a transaction or a meaningful engagement action. If our MAU definition is loose, the flat number may be hiding a real active user base that is actually smaller than we think.\n\nThird I segment the MAU trend by acquisition channel and cohort. Users acquired through the 30% increased marketing spend may have fundamentally different behavior from organic users. If the new cohorts are dragging down the average, I know the marketing is acquiring users who were never going to be genuinely active regardless of what the product does.\n\nFourth I look at what competitors changed in the last four months. Flat growth in a growing market is relative decline. If a competitor launched cashback or a UPI feature that we do not have, the market may be actively churning toward them.\n\nI come to my VP with a hypothesis ranked by confidence, not a list of possibilities. My recommendation will either be a product intervention, an acquisition channel change, or a value proposition reframe depending on where the diagnostic points. What I do not recommend is increasing marketing spend further until we understand why the current spend is not converting to lasting active users.`,
