        q: "You are managing a SIM swap fraud prevention feature rollout at a telecom. During testing you discover that your fraud detection model has a 12% false positive rate, meaning it blocks legitimate SIM swap requests from genuine customers. Launch is three weeks away. How do you handle this?",
        a: `A 12% false positive rate on a SIM swap fraud prevention feature is not a launch blocker by itself, but it is a serious product quality problem that I need to address before we launch.\n\nSIM swap fraud prevention exists to protect customers from account takeovers, which cause severe harm. A system that catches fraudulent SIM swaps but occasionally blocks legitimate ones is better than no system at all. But 12% is too high to be acceptable without a well designed fallback for false positive customers.\n\nThe first thing I do is understand the composition of the false positives. Which customer characteristics are triggering the false positive most frequently? Is it customers traveling internationally? Customers who recently changed their device? Customers with unusual call patterns? Understanding the false positive profile tells me whether this is a model tuning problem that can be addressed in three weeks or a fundamental data quality issue that will take longer.\n\nIf the false positive rate can be reduced to below 5% through model tuning in the next two weeks, I target that improvement and reassess before making a launch decision.\n\nIf the false positive rate cannot be meaningfully reduced in three weeks, I design a robust exception handling process as the condition for launch. Every blocked SIM swap generates an immediate notification to the customer via an alternative channel, a clear explanation that their request requires additional verification, and a fast path to verification through our customer care system. The exception path needs to resolve a blocked legitimate request within two to four hours, not two to four days.\n\nI also set a post launch monitoring requirement. I want daily visibility on the false positive rate in production, because model performance in a test environment can differ from production behavior. If the false positive rate in production exceeds 15%, I want a pre agreed rollback or intervention protocol in place.\n\nWhat I do not do is launch a fraud prevention feature that blocks 12% of legitimate customers without a tested, fast exception handling path. A customer whose legitimate SIM swap is blocked during a phone upgrade or international travel and cannot get resolution quickly becomes a complaint, a churn risk, and potentially a TRAI complaint.`,
