        q: "You are a PM at a fintech and your product's Net Promoter Score has dropped 15 points over the past quarter. Your leadership wants a root cause analysis. How do you structure it?",
        a: `A 15 point NPS drop in a quarter is a significant signal but NPS as a single metric tells you almost nothing about root cause. The analysis has to go several layers deeper before you have anything actionable to present to leadership.\n\nI start by segmenting the NPS movement. A 15 point average drop can be caused by a large movement among a small segment or a small movement distributed evenly across all segments. I look at the NPS movement broken down by customer segment, acquisition channel, product usage pattern, and tenure. If the drop is concentrated in a specific cohort, the root cause is almost certainly something that affected that cohort specifically during the quarter. If it is uniform, the cause is likely something that affected the entire product experience.\n\nThe next layer is correlating the NPS movement with product and operational events during the quarter. What changed in the product? Were there releases that introduced new flows or removed existing ones? Were there reliability incidents? Were there pricing changes? Were there customer service changes? I map the NPS movement timeline against the event timeline and look for correlations.\n\nI also look at the verbatim feedback from detractors who drove the NPS decline. NPS surveys with open text fields contain the most direct signal and patterns in verbatim feedback are often faster to read than quantitative analysis. If 40% of new detractors mention the same specific feature or experience, that is your root cause.\n\nThe root cause analysis I present to leadership has three components. Here is what the data shows happened, specifically which segments moved and when. Here is the most likely cause based on the correlation analysis and the verbatim feedback. And here is what I recommend we do about it, which is a specific intervention with a specific expected impact on NPS over the next quarter. A root cause analysis that ends with a problem statement and no recommendation is an incomplete deliverable.`,
