        q: "You are a PM at a bank and your product team is demoralized after a major launch failed to meet expectations. How do you rebuild team confidence and momentum?",
        a: `Demoralized teams after a launch failure need honesty more than they need motivation and the worst thing a PM can do is launch a positivity campaign that glosses over what happened.\n\nThe first conversation I have with the team is a structured retrospective that is honest about what went wrong without assigning blame. The goal of the retrospective is not catharsis. It is learning. What specifically did not meet expectations? Was it the product quality, the market response, the internal execution, or the measurement approach? Each of these has different implications for what we do next and treating the failure as a single undifferentiated event prevents the team from learning from the specific things that went wrong.\n\nI also acknowledge my own role in what happened. If the requirements were unclear, if the launch timeline was unrealistic, if the success metrics were poorly defined, those are PM failures and I say so explicitly. A team that sees their PM own their failures honestly extends more trust to that PM's future judgment than one that sees blame distributed to everyone except the PM.\n\nAfter the retrospective, momentum comes from an early win, not from a motivational conversation. I look for the smallest meaningful improvement we can make and ship quickly. A team that ships something that works and that users respond positively to experiences a version of success that rebuilds confidence more effectively than any speech. The win does not have to be large. It has to be real.\n\nI also have individual conversations with each team member to understand how they are experiencing the situation personally. Demoralization after a failure is not uniform and what one person needs is different from what another needs. Some people need recognition that their specific contribution was valuable even when the overall outcome was not. Some need clarity about what comes next. Understanding what each person needs prevents me from treating the team as a monolith when their individual experiences are different.`,
