        q: "You are a PM at an insurtech and your product team has delivered a feature on time and on budget but early user data shows very low adoption. How do you respond?",
        a: `Low adoption after an on-time on-budget delivery is telling you the team executed well against the wrong requirements, and the metric the team should feel proud of is adoption, not delivery.\n\nI do not let the team mistake delivery for success. On time and on budget are inputs. User adoption is the output that matters and a feature that was delivered perfectly but that users do not use has not created value. Calibrating the team's success metrics to delivery outputs rather than user outcomes creates a team that gets very good at shipping things nobody uses.\n\nThe diagnostic for low adoption follows the same framework as any other adoption problem. Is the feature not being discovered, is it being discovered but not tried, or is it being tried but not repeated? Each of these has a different intervention.\n\nIf the feature is not being discovered, the information architecture or the launch communication failed. The feature may be buried in a navigation path that users do not follow, or the launch may have relied on users finding it rather than proactively introducing it.\n\nIf the feature is being discovered but not tried, the value proposition is not clear at the moment of discovery. The feature name, the description, or the entry point is not communicating enough about what the user will get from trying it.\n\nIf the feature is being tried but not repeated, the first experience is not delivering on the implied promise. This is the most common scenario and it requires looking at the first-use experience specifically, not the overall feature design.\n\nI bring the team into this diagnostic rather than doing it in isolation. The engineers and designers who built the feature have hypotheses about why adoption is low and those hypotheses are often right. A team that diagnoses its own adoption problem and iterates to fix it learns more than one that is handed a diagnosis from the PM.`,
