        q: "You are a PM at a telecom and you have identified a significant market opportunity but your company's current strategy does not include it. How do you make the case for pursuing it?",
        a: `Making a case for an opportunity that sits outside the current strategy requires understanding why the current strategy does not include it before you present your case, because the most common reason an obvious opportunity is not in the strategy is that someone has already evaluated it and decided against it for reasons that may not be visible to you.\n\nI start by investigating the history. Was this opportunity previously evaluated and rejected? If so, why? What has changed since then that makes the opportunity more compelling now? Presenting an opportunity that was rejected 18 months ago without acknowledging that history and explaining what is different now signals to leadership that you have not done the work.\n\nIf the opportunity is genuinely new or the context has changed materially, I build the case in the language of the strategic framework the company already uses. A case that argues for pursuing an opportunity on its standalone merits is weaker than one that shows how the opportunity extends or accelerates the existing strategy. Leadership that has committed to a strategy is more receptive to additions that support it than to alternatives that compete with it.\n\nThe case I build has four specific elements. The size of the opportunity with a credible source, not an internal estimate. Our specific right to win in this space compared to the alternatives that exist or will exist. The investment required and the timeline to return. And what we are not doing if we pursue this, because every strategic addition has an opportunity cost and acknowledging that cost proactively builds credibility.\n\nI also choose the moment for the conversation deliberately. A case made during a budget cycle when resources are being allocated has more traction than one made mid-year when the budget is fixed. A case made after a competitor has moved into the space has more urgency than one made when the opportunity is still theoretical. Timing the conversation to when the decision maker is most receptive is not manipulation. It is effective communication.`,
