        q: "You are a PM at a fintech and your product has strong product market fit in one city but is struggling in a second city where you recently expanded. How do you diagnose and address this?",
        a: `Struggling in a second city after strong performance in the first is almost never a marketing problem and almost always a product market fit problem specific to the second city's context. The instinct to increase marketing spend in the second city is expensive and rarely effective.\n\nThe diagnostic starts with understanding what is genuinely different about the second city. This is not a rhetorical question. The differences that matter for a fintech product are specific: the demographic composition of the target market, the existing financial infrastructure customers are used to, the competitive alternatives available, the income profile and savings behavior patterns, the regulatory environment if it varies by state, and the specific payment and banking behaviors that are common in that geography.\n\nI look at the funnel data for the second city compared to the first. Where specifically does performance diverge? If acquisition is fine but activation is low, the product experience is not landing for second city users in the same way it did for first city users. If acquisition is low, the product or its positioning is not reaching the right people through the channels that work in the second city. If activation is comparable but retention is low, the product is not delivering ongoing value in a way that fits the second city user's context.\n\nI also talk to second city users who did not activate or did not retain, which is harder than talking to satisfied users but more informative. A user who tried the product and stopped can tell me specifically what did not work. A user who never got started can tell me what the barrier was. Ten of those conversations are worth more than any quantitative funnel analysis for understanding a market fit gap.\n\nIf the analysis reveals that the second city requires a meaningfully different product or distribution approach, I make that case to leadership with specific investment requirements. A second city expansion that requires 80% of the same product and 20% localization is a different decision from one that requires a fundamentally different approach. Knowing which one you are facing before you commit additional resources is the purpose of the diagnostic.`,
